Use a JOIN — you link attendance_records.faculty_id to faculty.id (must match on organization_id too):
Basic lookup query for your app:
SELECT ar.id AS record_id,
       ar.faculty_id,
       f.full_name,
       f.email,
       d.name AS department,
       ar.attendance_date,
       ar.status,
       ar.first_entry_time,
       ar.last_exit_time,
       ar.total_inside_minutes
FROM attendance_records ar
JOIN faculty f
     ON f.id = ar.faculty_id
    AND f.organization_id = ar.organization_id
LEFT JOIN departments d ON d.id = f.department_id
WHERE ar.attendance_date = CURRENT_DATE      -- "live" = today only
ORDER BY ar.first_entry_time DESC;
Variations you'll likely need:
- Who is currently inside right now:
... WHERE ar.attendance_date = CURRENT_DATE
      AND ar.last_exit_time IS NULL
- Latest activity feed (poll this every few seconds):
... ORDER BY ar.updated_at DESC LIMIT 50
- One specific person's history (parameterize in your code):
... WHERE ar.faculty_id = $1
Tips:
- Always include the organization_id match in the join — the FK is composite, so it's both correct and faster.
- In your app code, pass IDs as parameters ($1, or ? depending on driver), never string-concatenate.
- Polling tip: add WHERE ar.updated_at > $1 (last poll time) to fetch only changed rows instead of everything.
- Indexes already exist for these lookups (faculty_id, attendance_date unique index), so it'll stay fast.
